Viva questions and answers
Q1. What is SVM?

SVM stands for Support Vector Machine. It is a supervised machine learning algorithm used for classification and regression.

Q2. What is the purpose of this practical?

To classify handwritten digits using the SVM algorithm.

Q3. What is classification?

Classification is the process of predicting a category or class from input data.

Q4. What is the Digits dataset?

It is a built-in Scikit-learn dataset containing handwritten digit images from 0 to 9.

Q5. What is the size of each digit image?

Each image is 8 × 8 pixels.

Q6. How many features are present in each image?

64 features (8 × 8 = 64).

Q7. What is SVC?

SVC stands for Support Vector Classifier. It is used to perform classification using SVM.

Q8. What is a kernel in SVM?

A kernel helps SVM handle data that cannot be separated using a straight line.

Q9. What is RBF?

RBF stands for Radial Basis Function. It is a nonlinear kernel used in SVM.

Q10. What is the use of fit()?

It trains the machine learning model.

Q11. What is the use of predict()?

It predicts output labels for new input data.

Q12. What is accuracy?

Accuracy measures the percentage of correct predictions.

Q13. What is precision?

Precision measures how many predicted positive samples are actually positive.

Q14. What is recall?

Recall measures how many actual positive samples are correctly identified.

Q15. What is F1-score?

F1-score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall.

Q16. What is the use of reshape(8,8)?

It converts 64 pixel values into an 8 × 8 image.

Q17. What is the use of cmap="gray"?

It displays the image in grayscale.

Q18. What is random_state=42?

It ensures reproducible random splitting of the dataset.

